Filed by langertha-llm-advisor, 2026-09-01, as the central finding of a concept red-team pass.
All provider claims below are from docs read 2026-09-01. No live calls were made.

## The observation

ADR 0002 derives the capability flags from the composed role inventory, with
`around engine_capabilities` as the sanctioned escape hatch for wire reality. Today the escape
hatch is used SIX times in the whole distribution (AnthropicBase, OpenAIBase, Gemini, MiniMax,
Moonshot, Perplexity) and none of those six touches a tool-choice or structured-output flag.

Dumping `engine_capabilities` across the OpenAI-dialect fleet gives literally one row, repeated:

    Groq Cerebras DeepSeek MiniMax Moonshot OllamaOpenAI LlamaCpp SGLang Scaleway
    Hetzner OpenRouter HuggingFace NousResearch vLLM XAI Mistral TSystems
      -> tool_choice_named=1 tool_choice_any=1 tool_choice_none=1
         response_format_json_schema=1 response_format_json_object=1
         tools_native=1 parallel_tool_use=1

(Perplexity is the single engine that differs, because it composes no `Role::Tools`.)

So on this axis the registry currently carries **zero discriminating information**. Every flag is
a restatement of "this engine composes Role::Tools + Role::ResponseFormat". `chat_f`'s
auto-rewrite matrix (ADR 0005) keys off exactly these flags, so it is deciding on a constant.

## Where the wire disagrees -- verified, with the failure mode

Loud (HTTP 400) unless noted; the SILENT ones are the dangerous half.

| Engine | Flag that is wrong | Reality | Source |
|---|---|---|---|
| **DeepSeek** | `response_format_json_schema` | `response_format.type` enum is **`[text, json_object]`** only. No `json_schema`. Schema enforcement needs a forced tool with `strict:true` on the `/beta` base URL. | api-docs.deepseek.com/api/create-chat-completion , /guides/json_mode |
| **Cerebras** | tools + response_format together | "**`tools` and `response_format` cannot be used in the same request**" (gpt-oss-120b hard-rejects) | inference-docs.cerebras.ai/capabilities/structured-outputs |
| **Groq** | tools + response_format together; also streaming | "Streaming and tool use are not currently supported with Structured Outputs." `strict:true` only on `openai/gpt-oss-20b`, `-120b`, `qwen/qwen3.8-27b`. | console.groq.com/docs/structured-outputs , /docs/tool-use |
| **Moonshot** | `tool_choice_named` on the DEFAULT model | "Forcing a specific tool is currently **incompatible with thinking**... returns a 400 error" -- and `kimi-k3` (the engine default) always thinks. Also `kimi-k2.6` / `kimi-k2.7-code` "do not support `required` and return an error if it is passed" -> `tool_choice_any` is wrong there too. | platform.kimi.ai/docs/guide/use-tool-choice |
| **MiniMax** | `tool_choice_*`, `response_format_*` | `tool_choice` is **absent entirely** from the current `/v1/chat/completions` schema (0 occurrences), as is `response_format` and `parallel_tool_calls`. The deprecated native endpoint had `enum:[none,auto]`. | platform.minimax.io/docs/api-reference/text-chat-openai + openapi-chat-openai.json |
| **Scaleway** | `tool_choice_any`; `parallel_tool_use`; `response_format_json_object` | `tool_choice` enum is **`none\|auto\|required`** -- no `any`. `parallel_tool_calls` is accepted but **inert**: "even if set `false` this parameter will be ignored and act as if set to `true`" (SILENT). `json_object` is "Deprecated... should not be used anymore". | scaleway.com/en/docs/generative-apis/ |
| **Ollama /v1** | `tool_choice_named`, `_any`, `_none` | `tool_choice` is **not supported at all** -- the doc's own support checklist reads `- [ ] tool_choice`, and the Go server has no `DisallowUnknownFields`, so it is accepted, ignored, and answered **HTTP 200** (SILENT). | docs.ollama.com/api/openai-compatibility |
| **llama.cpp** | `tool_choice_named` | The parser reads `tool_choice` as a `std::string`, so the object form is **silently downgraded to `"auto"`** (SILENT). `strict` is ignored. | ggml-org/llama.cpp `common/chat.cpp`, `tools/server/README.md` |
| **SGLang** | `tool_choice_auto`, `_none` | Only the named form and the grammar backends are documented; `auto`/`none` are **not listed**, and named needs the `xgrammar` backend ("may not be fully supported with outlines"). `strict` undocumented. | docs.sglang.io/docs/advanced_features/tool_parser |
| **Hetzner** | `tools_native`, all `tool_choice_*`, `response_format_*` | Tool calling and structured output are **documented nowhere** on Hetzner's inference page. The platform is explicitly experimental: "you should not use the platform for production environments." Only `/v1/models`, `/v1/completions`, `/v1/chat/completions` exist. | docs.hetzner.com/general/company-and-policy/experiments/inference/ |
| **NousResearch** | `tools_native` + `tools_hermes` both set | Engine composes BOTH `Role::Tools` and `Role::HermesTools` while `tool_wire_format` is `hermes`, so `supports('tools_native')` is true for a prompt-injection wire. Separately: the hosted catalog (`inference-api.nousresearch.com/v1`, 376 models) contains **zero Hermes models**, and `default_model` is `Hermes-4-70B`. | live public /v1/models catalog; huggingface.co/NousResearch/Hermes-4-405B |
| **Anthropic family** | `tool_choice_named`, `_any` | 400 on `claude-fable-5-1` / `claude-mythos-5-1` and under manual extended thinking. See k133. | platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/tool-reference |

Two of these directly contradict **ADR 0005**, whose premise is that structured output and forced
tool calling are two faces of one mechanism that can be rewritten into each other: on Groq and
Cerebras they are **mutually exclusive in one request**, so a caller passing both `tools` and
`response_format` to `chat_f` builds a body the provider rejects. `chat_f` has no rule for
"these two are exclusive here" because no flag can express it.

## What the market is doing instead -- and it is worth copying

Two providers already publish machine-readable per-model capability metadata, free and unauth'd:

- **OpenRouter** `GET https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models` -> `supported_parameters[]` per model.
  Across the 419 live models: `tools` on 353, `tool_choice` on 345, `structured_outputs` on 335,
  `response_format` on 360, `reasoning_effort` on 151, `parallel_tool_calls` on **7**. Finer still:
  `GET /api/v1/models/{author}/{slug}/endpoints` gives per-PROVIDER
  `supports_tool_choice: {none, auto, required, function}`, and two endpoints for the same model
  genuinely diverge. There is also `provider: {require_parameters: true}` to refuse routing to a
  provider that lacks a parameter -- and without it, unsupported params are **silently ignored**
  ("providers that don't support all the LLM parameters specified in your request can still
  receive the request, but will ignore unknown parameters").
- **HuggingFace** `GET https://router.huggingface.co/v1/models` -> per provider
  `supports_tools` (bool) and `supports_structured_output` (bool), plus `context_length`.
  NOTE the negative: the Hub API (`?expand[]=inferenceProviderMapping`) does NOT carry these --
  only the router does.
- **AKI** publishes a per-model tool table in prose (with an undefined "Basic Support" tier -- k132).
- **Anthropic** `GET /v1/models` gained `max_input_tokens`, `max_tokens` and **`capabilities`**
  (changelog 2026-03-18).

## The design question this ticket exists to raise

`supports($cap)` is engine-scoped. The wire is model-scoped, and increasingly provider-scoped
below the model (OpenRouter). Langertha already model-gates in three places, each invented
independently and none of them named:

  - `Engine::OpenAI::_max_tokens_key`      -- `$model =~ /\Agpt-5/ ? 'max_completion_tokens' : 'max_tokens'`
  - `Langertha::Reasoning::to_gemini_level` -- per-model-family level clamps
  - `Langertha::Reasoning::_is_fable_class` -- `/fable|mythos/i` -> omit the thinking field
  - (and `DeepSeek::reasoning_kwargs`, which ADR 0009 decision 5 already sanctions as the
    "divergence below the tag's resolution" escape hatch)

Options, cheapest first, all needing a maintainer call:

1. **Do nothing structural; spend the escape hatch.** Add ~10 `around engine_capabilities`
   corrections from the table above. Cheap, honest, no new concept -- but it cannot express the
   per-model cases (Moonshot k3 vs k2.6, Anthropic fable vs sonnet) or the pairwise exclusions
   (Groq/Cerebras tools XOR response_format), which are the ones that 400.
2. **Make `supports` model-aware**: `supports($cap, $model)` with the engine's `chat_model` as the
   default, and a per-engine `%MODEL_CAPS` override table. Follows ADR 0002's spirit, extends its
   resolution to where the wire actually lives. Biggest surface change.
3. **A `Langertha::Capability` value object** with a third state -- yes / no / **unverified** --
   so Hetzner's undocumented tools and AKI's "Basic Support" are not silently rounded up to yes.
4. **Live capability discovery** for the providers that publish it (OpenRouter, HuggingFace,
   Anthropic `/v1/models`), cached like `Role::Models` already caches the model list.
5. **Model pairwise exclusions** are a separate shape from a flag; if `chat_f` is to stay
   trustworthy it needs at minimum a per-engine `croak` when tools and response_format are
   combined on Groq / Cerebras, rather than shipping a body that 400s.

Recommendation: option 1 NOW for the loud cases plus the three silent ones (Ollama /v1,
llama.cpp, Scaleway `parallel_tool_calls`), because a silently-ignored `tool_choice` is worse
than a 400 -- the caller believes the tool was forced. Then decide 2 vs 3 deliberately, and
record it as an ADR amending 0002. Do NOT do 4 first: it makes the engine construction path
network-dependent.

## What is NOT wrong

Verified correct and worth not "fixing": Perplexity really still has no tool calling (see k138 for
its EOL); xAI documents named tool_choice, structured outputs and `parallel_tool_calls` on every
text model; Mistral accepts named tool_choice plus `any`/`required` synonyms and `json_schema`
with `strict`; vLLM supports named tool_choice by default with no flag (only `auto` needs
`--enable-auto-tool-choice`); T-Systems types `tools`/`tool_choice`/`response_format` as
passthrough, so the flags follow whatever backend the model id routes to.
